Make upstream's register file simulate under GHDL, and give it the same
power-up state as the two implementations it is being compared against.

Applied by "make crosscheck_rtl" to the copy of QNICE-FPGA's vhdl/ that it
extracts at the pinned commit (the Makefile's QNICE_REF). Nothing here is
committed upstream, nothing here touches anybody's checkout, and this is the
ONLY upstream file that is modified -- qnice_cpu.vhd, alu.vhd, alu_shifter.vhd,
cpu_constants.vhd and EAE.vhd are all analysed exactly as they ship. If the
patch stops applying, upstream has moved and this needs re-deriving rather than
forcing; "patch" fails the build loudly, which is the intent.

Three changes, none of which alters what the register file does once the
simulation is running.

1. Zero the register arrays at elaboration.

   This is the one change that is about the comparison rather than about GHDL.
   Upstream's register file has no reset -- R0..R12 come up undefined -- while
   this repo's CPU brings them up at zero (src/sub/dp_ram.vhd starts an
   uninitialised array at zero) and so does the emulator (qnice.c's
   reset_machine() zeroes gbl$registers explicitly). The QNICE ISA does not
   define power-up register contents, so all three are within their rights;
   but a differential test has to start the two sides in the same
   architectural state, exactly as it loads them the same program.
   test/prog_simple.asm is the program that notices: it opens with
   "MOVE R2, R6 / MOVE R3, @R6" on registers it never initialises, which is a
   store to address 0 on a machine whose registers start at zero and a store to
   nowhere in particular on one whose registers start at 'U'.

2. Give sel_rbank_i an initial value.

   Every VHDL process runs once at time zero, reading the initial values of its
   inputs. sel_rbank_i is a plain "integer", so that initial value is
   integer'left, and the read processes below index the register array with
   "sel_rbank_i + <register number>" -- which overflows the integer type before
   it is ever used as an index. GHDL checks that and aborts the run at 0 ns.

3. Index the read processes from read_addrN rather than from read_addrN_i.

   read_addrN_i and is_upper_register_rdN are separate signals, computed from
   read_addrN by concurrent assignments, so within a delta cycle they lag it:
   the process can select the "special register" branch on the NEW read_addrN
   while indexing with the OLD read_addrN_i. That is transient and harmless in
   hardware -- the CPU samples read_dataN on a clock edge, long after the
   network has settled -- but the stale index is out of the (13 to 15) array's
   bounds, and GHDL aborts on it rather than letting it settle. Reading the
   address signal directly inside the process makes the branch and the index
   consistent by construction; the steady-state function is unchanged, and so
   is the synthesised result. is_upper_register_rd1/rd2 become unused, and
   upstream's bit-pattern trick for "is this 8..12" is written out as the
   comparison it stands for.

--- a/vhdl/register_file.vhd
+++ b/vhdl/register_file.vhd
@@ -74,14 +74,14 @@
